= a
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Work on a <filtered probability space> $(\Omega,\mathcal F,(\mathcal F_n)_{n\geq0},\mathbb P)$. A <submartingale> is a real-valued <stochastic process> satisfying the following three conditions: $Y_n$ is $\mathcal F_n$-measurable, $\mathbb E|Y_n|<\infty$, and
$$
\boxed{\mathbb E[Y_{n+1}\mid\mathcal F_n]\geq Y_n\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$
Thus it is <adapted>, has <integrable random variables> at every time, and its conditional expected next value is at least its current value. Repeated conditioning gives $\mathbb E[Y_m\mid\mathcal F_n]\geq Y_n$ for every $m\geq n$. **The inequality concerns <conditional expectation>; individual <sample paths> need not increase.** If no <filtration> is specified, the definition can be applied to the <natural filtration> generated by the process.

= b
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

The <almost sure submartingale convergence theorem> says that if a <submartingale> $(Y_n)$ satisfies
$$
\sup_n\mathbb E[Y_n^+]<\infty,
$$
then there is an <integrable random variable> $Y_\infty$ such that
$$
\boxed{Y_n\longrightarrow Y_\infty\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$
Indeed the positive-part bound also bounds the negative parts, since $\mathbb E Y_n\geq\mathbb E Y_0$; the theorem gives a finite limit. In particular, the <martingale convergence theorem> applies to any <martingale> with $\sup_n\mathbb E|M_n|<\infty$.

For a nonnegative <martingale>, $\mathbb E M_n=\mathbb E M_0$ supplies this bound automatically. Its limit obeys $\mathbb E M_\infty\leq\mathbb E M_0$ by the <Fatou lemma>. **<Almost sure convergence> alone need not preserve the <expectation> or give convergence in L1.** <Uniform integrability> is the additional condition that supplies <convergence in L1> and the identity $M_n=\mathbb E[M_\infty\mid\mathcal F_n]$.

= c
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Take <independent and identically distributed random variables> $\xi_k$ with the <Bernoulli distribution> of parameter $1/2$, and their <natural filtration>. Set
$$
\boxed{M_n=2^n\mathbf1_{\{\xi_1=\cdots=\xi_n=1\}},\qquad M_0=1.}
$$
The event at $n=0$ is the whole sample space. <Independence> gives
$$
\mathbb E[M_{n+1}\mid\mathcal F_n]
=2^{n+1}\mathbf1_{\{\xi_1=\cdots=\xi_n=1\}}\,\frac12=M_n,
$$
so this is a nonnegative <martingale>; it is integrable with $\mathbb E M_n=1$. The first zero among the $\xi_k$ is almost surely finite, since the probability of having only ones through time $n$ is $2^{-n}\to0$. After that first zero, every $M_n$ is zero. Hence **the almost sure limit is zero**:
$$
\boxed{M_n\longrightarrow0\quad\text{almost surely},\qquad\mathbb E M_n=1.}
$$
This <fair-coin doubling martingale> also exhibits failure of <uniform integrability>: <convergence in L1> would force the <expectations> to converge to zero.

= d
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

Let $(\mathcal F_n)$ be the <natural filtration> of the <random walk on a graph>. For a bounded <harmonic function on a graph>, the transition rule and the <Markov property> give
$$
\mathbb E[h(X_{n+1})\mid\mathcal F_n]
=\frac1{\deg(X_n)}\sum_{u\sim X_n}h(u)=h(X_n).
$$
Thus $h(X_n)$ is a bounded <martingale>. The <martingale convergence theorem> gives a finite almost sure limit $L$.

On the probability-one recurrence event, every vertex $v$ is visited along a sequence $n_k\to\infty$. Along that sequence $h(X_{n_k})=h(v)$, while convergence of the whole sequence forces $h(v)=L$. Therefore every pair of vertices has the same value. This is the <bounded harmonic function theorem on a recurrent graph>:
$$
\boxed{h(v)=h(w)\quad\text{for all vertices }v,w.}
$$
**Recurrence makes each vertex value a subsequential limit of the same convergent martingale.** A connected <locally finite graph> is countable, since it is the union of its finite distance balls, so these almost sure assertions can also be combined by a countable intersection. The one-vertex case is immediate.

= e
{parent=1}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

Write $D_k=M_k-M_{k-1}$ for the <martingale difference sequence>. <Bounded increments> mean that one deterministic $C<\infty$ satisfies $|D_k|\leq C$ for every $k$, almost surely. Then $\mathbb E D_k=0$ and, for $j<k$,
$$
\mathbb E[D_jD_k]
=\mathbb E\left[D_j\mathbb E[D_k\mid\mathcal F_{k-1}]\right]=0.
$$
The increments are <uncorrelated random variables>, uniformly bounded in $L^2$. Applying the supplied <strong law for uniformly L2-bounded uncorrelated random variables> gives $n^{-1}\sum_{k=1}^nD_k\to0$ almost surely. Also $M_0/n\to0$, because $M_0$ is finite almost surely. Therefore **a martingale with <bounded increments> has zero linear growth**:
$$
\boxed{\frac{M_n}{n}\longrightarrow0\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$
This is the <strong law for martingales with bounded increments>.

For the <submartingale>, let $\Delta Y_k=Y_k-Y_{k-1}$ and define its predictable drift and compensator by
$$
a_k=\mathbb E[\Delta Y_k\mid\mathcal F_{k-1}]\geq0,
\qquad A_n=\sum_{k=1}^na_k,
\qquad N_n=Y_n-A_n.
$$
This is the <Doob decomposition in discrete time>. The process $N_n$ is a <martingale>. Since $|\Delta Y_k|\leq C$, we have $0\leq a_k\leq C$, so $|N_k-N_{k-1}|\leq2C$. The result just proved gives $N_n/n\to0$ almost surely, and $A_n\geq0$. Consequently the <strong law for submartingales with bounded increments> is
$$
\boxed{\liminf_{n\to\infty}\frac{Y_n}{n}\geq0\quad\text{almost surely}.}
$$
The compensator may grow, so the conclusion is a lower bound rather than a claim of convergence to zero.

